WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.593
A practical Bayesian network workflow begins with a clear reasoning question.

00:00:04.593 --> 00:00:08.270
Are you supporting equipment diagnosis, weather-related risk, fraud review,

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:11.537
security alert triage, project risk, or system diagnostics?

00:00:11.537 --> 00:00:13.171
The question defines scope.

00:00:13.171 --> 00:00:13.988
Without scope,

00:00:13.988 --> 00:00:18.890
it is easy to add variables because they are interesting rather than

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:20.932
because they support a decision.

00:00:20.932 --> 00:00:22.975
Next, identify relevant uncertain variables.

00:00:22.975 --> 00:00:24.609
Represent them as nodes.

00:00:24.609 --> 00:00:26.651
Draw an acyclic dependency graph.

00:00:26.651 --> 00:00:29.102
Define parents, children, ancestors, and descendants.

00:00:29.102 --> 00:00:30.736
Check conditional independence assumptions:

00:00:30.736 --> 00:00:35.637
does each variable depend directly on the variables you chose as parents,

00:00:35.637 --> 00:00:39.722
and not on unrelated non-descendants once those parents are known?

00:00:39.722 --> 00:00:43.399
The graph should make assumptions visible enough for review.

00:00:43.399 --> 00:00:45.441
Then define local probability distributions.

00:00:45.441 --> 00:00:47.075
Root nodes receive priors.

00:00:47.075 --> 00:00:51.160
Child nodes receive conditional probability tables or other conditional distributions.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:55.653
The graph allows the joint distribution to factor into local terms.

00:00:55.653 --> 00:00:59.329
Evidence can then be entered, queries can be asked,

00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:01.780
and posterior probabilities can be computed.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:05.865
The model may support predictive reasoning from causes to effects,

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:11.175
diagnostic reasoning from effects to causes, and intercausal reasoning such as explaining away.

00:01:11.175 --> 00:01:13.626
Finally, validate and maintain the model.

00:01:13.626 --> 00:01:14.443
Test scenarios.

00:01:14.443 --> 00:01:15.260
Check calibration.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:16.485
Run sensitivity analysis.

00:01:16.485 --> 00:01:19.753
Document assumptions, probability sources, missing-data behavior, limitations, versions,

00:01:19.753 --> 00:01:21.387
owners, and review criteria.

00:01:21.387 --> 00:01:25.472
Monitor for model drift and revise when the environment changes.

00:01:25.472 --> 00:01:28.331
A Bayesian network does not eliminate uncertainty.

00:01:28.331 --> 00:01:32.824
Its value is that it makes uncertain assumptions and relationships explicit,

00:01:32.824 --> 00:01:37.317
so they can be reviewed, updated, tested, and used more carefully.

00:01:37.317 --> 00:01:42.628
The course has covered the major pieces: nodes, edges, priors, conditional probabilities, CPTs,

00:01:42.628 --> 00:01:45.487
factorization, evidence, inference, posterior updating, predictive reasoning,

00:01:45.487 --> 00:01:48.755
diagnostic reasoning, intercausal reasoning, explaining away, parameter learning,

00:01:48.755 --> 00:01:51.206
structure learning, validation, and responsible use.

00:01:51.206 --> 00:01:52.840
Keep the workflow practical.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:57.741
Start with the decision, model only what is needed, document uncertainty honestly,

00:01:57.741 --> 00:02:03.460
and revise the network when evidence shows the assumptions are no longer good enough.
